            Think of the RSI as a speedometer that measures how fast car prices are moving, and whether buyers or sellers are getting tired. It ranges from a score of 0 to 100, 
            It tells if the market is over-pumped (Overbought) or completely exhausted (Oversold).
               - RSI Settings: 14-period.
                   Sell Condition: When Price Touches Identified Entry Line, The RSI line must rise above 70 (overbought). Wait for the RSI line to cross back below 70. This confirms that buying pressure is failing and sellers are taking control.
                   Buy Condition: When Price Touches Identified Entry Line. The RSI line must dip below 30 (oversold). Wait for the RSI line to cross back above 30. This confirms that selling momentum has run out and buyers are stepping back in.

       - 2. Stochastic Oscillator (Stoch). (M15 To M5, At Least 1 Of 2 Timeframes Needed To Satisfy Stochastic Oscillator Indicator Confirmation)
               - Stoch Settings: Default (14, 3, 3) or faster (5, 3, 3).
                   Sell Condition: When Price Touches Identified Entry Line, Both lines must rise above 80 (extreme overbought). The sell trigger occurs when the %K line crosses under the %D line while inside or just exiting this zone.
                   Buy Condition: When Price Touches Identified Entry Line, Both the %K (fast) and %D (slow) lines must dip below 20 (extreme oversold). The buy trigger occurs when the %K line crosses above the %D line while inside or just exiting this zone.

    > Trend & Structure Alignment Indicators. These indicators ensures do not "catch a falling knife." They prove that market structure is actually shifting in favor before we commit capital :

       - 3. Exponential Moving Average (EMA50). (H1 To M15, At Least 1 Of 2 Timeframes Needed To Satisfy EMA50 Indicator Confirmation. And M5 Only For Lower-Timeframe Candle Confirming)
            Think of the EMA 50 as a moving floor or ceiling. It takes the average price of the last 50 candles but puts more weight on the most recent candles so it reacts quickly to sudden news or movements.
            It acts as a visual trend barrier. If the price is above it, the immediate trend is up. If the price is below it, the immediate trend is down.
               - Settings: 50-EMA (medium-term trend) and 200-EMA (long-term trend).
                    The Bounce Filter : Identified Entry Lines are theoretical area where price might stop. The EMA 50 gives it a physical barrier.
                    Sell Condition :  Wait for the price to hit Entry Line, struggle to go higher, and then close a candle completely below the EMA 50 line. This acts as proof that the "ceiling" held.
                                The 50-EMA must be tracking below the 200-EMA. Once price taps Entry Line, wait for a lower-timeframe M5 candlestick to close cleanly below the 50-EMA.
                                Alternatively, look for the price to reject the 200-EMA as structural resistance within Entry Line Area.
                    Buy Condition :  Wait for the price to hit Entry Line, struggle to go lower, and then close a candle completely above M15 EMA 50 line. This acts as proof that the "floor" held.
                               The 50-EMA must be tracking above the 200-EMA. Once price taps Entry Line, wait for a lower-timeframe M5 candlestick to close cleanly above the 50-EMA.
                               Alternatively, look for the price to reject the 200-EMA as structural support within Entry Line Area.

       - 4. Moving Average Convergence Divergence (MACD). (H1 To M15, At Least 1 Of 2 Timeframes Needed To Satisfy MACD Indicator Confirmation).
            Think of the MACD as a momentum camera. It doesn't look at individual price ticks; it looks at the overall shifting weight of the market,
            It consists of two lines and a bar chart (the histogram) that moves above and below a central "0" line.  It filters out "fake" price movements and tells you when the actual shift in power happens.
               - Settings: Standard (12, 26, 9).
                   Sell Condition : The easiest rule is the central 0 line. In A Sell Condition trade, When Price Touches Identified Entry Line, wait for the MACD bars to switch from being above 0 (green/positive) to printing below 0 (red/negative),
                                    Or While Price Touches Identified Entry Line, the green MACD histogram bars should begin shrinking toward the zero line. The official sell signal is triggered when the blue MACD line crosses below the orange Signal line above the zero axis.
                   Buy Condition : The easiest rule is the central 0 line. In A Buy Condition trade, When Price Touches Identified Entry Line, wait for the MACD bars to switch from being below 0 (red/negative) to printing above 0 (green/positive).
                                    Or While price Touches Identified Entry Line, look at the MACD histogram. The red bars should begin shrinking toward the zero line. The official buy signal is triggered when the blue MACD line crosses above the orange Signal line below the zero axis.

